= Jujube Dates

_Scope:_ This entry treats the *jujube* proper — _Ziziphus jujuba_ Mill. (Rhamnaceae) — the *Chinese / temperate jujube, red date, dazao*. This is *not* the date palm fruit (_Phoenix dactylifera_, Arecaceae — see _medjool-dates.typ_). The fruits and the date palm fruits share an English name but are botanically unrelated; the dietetic and pharmacological profiles are also distinct, and the classical traditions treat them entirely separately. The Indian jujube _Z. mauritiana_ Lam. (the *ber* of South Asia) and the *sidr* of the Arabian peninsula _Z. spina-christi_ (L.) Desf. are noted comparatively in the Nomenclature section — each closely related and partly overlapping in classical reception, but distinct species. The seed of the *sour jujube* _Z. jujuba_ var. _spinosa_ (酸棗仁 _suānzǎorén_) is a *distinct canonical Chinese drug* used for *insomnia*, and is noted as a sibling drug, not the principal subject of this entry. Status: classical in all four canonical traditions — Chinese (大棗/紅棗, _Shennong Bencao Jing_ top grade), Ayurveda (_badara_, Charaka), Tibb (_ʿunnāb_, Avicennan), and with Qurʾānic / Prophetic-medicine attestation via the genus (_sidr_ / _Sidrat al-Muntahā_, sūrat al-Najm 53:14–16).

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Ziziphus jujuba_ Mill. (= _Z. vulgaris_ Lam.; = _Z. sativa_ Gaertn.), family Rhamnaceae. The principal drug-and-food is the dried, sun-cured *ripe fruit* (_Ziziphi fructus_ in modern Chinese pharmacognosy). Latin _ziziphus_ from Greek ζίζυφον (_zízyphon_), itself from a Persian / Iranian source (Middle Persian _zayfū_ / _zedfak_), tracing further to Akkadian _supurginnu_ or related Mesopotamian Semitic. The Greek-Latin-Arabic line is one of the great Silk-Road borrowing chains — the fruit's Mediterranean reception is via Iran and the trade routes, not direct domestication. The species epithet _jujuba_ is a Latinization back from medieval Latin _jujuba_ < Greek ζίζυφον.#footnote[Etymology: Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _zizyphum_); Beekes, _Etymological Dictionary of Greek_ (s.v. _zízyphon_) — Greek loanword from an Iranian source; Mayrhofer for the wider Indo-European context. The Akkadian / Mesopotamian Semitic line is suggestive; the precise ultimate origin is unsettled.]

*English:* _Jujube_ (often pronounced as one or two syllables, with the two-syllable pronunciation closer to the medieval Latin); also _Chinese date_, _red date_, _Chinese jujube_. From Middle English _juiubes_ (15th c.), from medieval Latin _jujuba_, from Greek ζίζυφον. The "date" in "Chinese date" is from the fruit's appearance and texture (when dried, resembling a small dried date palm fruit) — *the English usage is a botanical false analogy*, since _Ziziphus_ (Rhamnaceae) and _Phoenix_ (Arecaceae) are unrelated.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "jujube, n."). The Chinese-date / date-palm-date confusion in English-language sources is a common error pattern, paralleled by the English "Chinese gooseberry" / kiwi-fruit terminology.]

*Chinese:* The Chinese nomenclature is rich and clinically central.
- *大棗 (dàzǎo)*, "great date" — the canonical drug name in the _Shennong Bencao Jing_ and the standard Chinese pharmacy term. Top-grade (上品) drug in the _Shennong_.
- *紅棗 (hóngzǎo)*, "red date" — the culinary / household term; functionally identical to 大棗.
- *黑棗 (hēizǎo)*, "black date" — the *steam-processed and sun-cured* variant; warming, more concentrated; some sources treat it as a distinct preparation, others as a process variant.
- *蜜棗 (mìzǎo)*, "honey date" — sugar-and-honey-impregnated preparation, sweetened candy register.
- *棗 (zǎo)* — the bare character; the ancient and broadest term.

The *seed* of the *sour jujube* — _Ziziphus jujuba_ var. _spinosa_ (sometimes treated as a distinct species _Z. spinosa_ Hu) — is a *separate canonical drug*: *酸棗仁 (suānzǎorén)*, "sour-date seed." This is the principal Chinese hypnotic / nervine drug (the active in 酸棗仁湯 _Suān Zǎo Rén Tāng_, the Han-dynasty insomnia formula). Sweet jujube fruit (大棗) and sour jujube seed (酸棗仁) are *separate drugs with different actions and channels* — the canonical materia-medica error is to confuse them.#footnote[大棗 in the _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (神農本草經, Han dynasty, top grade 上品); systematized in Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004). 酸棗仁 as a distinct canonical drug: separate entries in _Shennong_, _Bencao Gangmu_, and the modern materia medica. The species-level taxonomy of "sour jujube" — _Z. jujuba_ var. _spinosa_ vs. _Z. spinosa_ as a separate species — is a standing modern botanical question; pharmaceutically the suānzǎorén drug is the seed of this thorny variety / species, regardless of the formal taxon.]

*Sanskrit:* The classical Indian term for jujube is *बदर (badara)* — Caraka, Suśruta, Bhāvaprakāśa. In Indian classical context *badara* most commonly refers to _Ziziphus mauritiana_ Lam. (the *Indian ber*, the South Asian jujube) rather than _Z. jujuba_ proper, although the classical commentators do not always distinguish; the closely related _Z. jujuba_ and _Z. mauritiana_ overlap in classical reception, with the temperate (jujuba) and tropical (mauritiana) ranges complementing each other across the Indian classical foodscape. Other classical Sanskrit names: *कर्कन्धु (karkandhu)* — the smaller-fruited variety; *कुवल (kuvala)* — the larger-fruited; *सौवीर (sauvīra)* — a specific named variety (the "sauvīra-mauntain" — Aṣṭāṅga Hṛdaya naming); *बादर (bādara)* — the adjectival form. The classical fresh/ripe and dried profiles are treated as having slightly different dietetic readings — fresh is sweeter and more cooling, dried is sweeter-still and more nourishing.#footnote[Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. _badara_, _karkandhu_, _kuvala_, _sauvīra_). Charaka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 — _badara_ in the _phala-varga_ (fruits) section; Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 46; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Āmrādi-phala-varga_. The classical _badara_ : _Z. mauritiana_ identification is the modern dravyaguna consensus (P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II), but the temperate Z. jujuba was also part of the classical Indian fruit-foodscape, especially in north Indian and Himalayan-foothill cuisines.]

*Arabic / Persian / Urdu (Tibb register):* *عُنّاب (_ʿunnāb_)* — the canonical Tibb / Greco-Arabic name; treated by Avicenna and Ibn al-Bayṭār as a major drug. Persian *عُنّاب (_ʿannāb_)*; Urdu *عناب (_unnāb_)*. The Tibb _ʿunnāb_ is identified primarily with *_Ziziphus jujuba_* (the temperate Asian jujube); the closely related *_Z. spina-christi_* (Christ's thorn jujube, the *sidr* of Arabia) is a distinct species treated under the name سدر (_sidr_) — both share much of the medical reception, with _ʿunnāb_ as the canonical *medicinal* drug and *sidr* as the canonical *Qurʾānic-sacred* tree. The Maghrebi and Yemeni folk classification treats them as functionally interchangeable in some preparations; the Avicennan formal classification treats them as separate.#footnote[The _ʿunnāb_ / _sidr_ distinction: Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn fī al-Ṭibb_, Book II (the _al-Adwiya al-Mufrada_ survey), with separate entries; Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ li-Mufradāt al-Adwiya wa-l-Aghdhiya_, similarly. The two species share a Tibb-relevant overlap in profile but are treated formally as distinct.]

*Hebrew / biblical:* No biblical attestation of _Ziziphus jujuba_ specifically. The biblical text references aromatic plants by name, but the jujube genus enters the Levantine record primarily through _Z. spina-christi_ in late-classical and rabbinic sources, where the species is identified as the candidate for the "lotus" of _lōṭ_ in some traditional commentaries (a contested identification, with several other candidates).

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

大棗 is one of the most canonical and most clinically central Chinese drugs. Top-grade (上品) in the _Shennong Bencao Jing_; appears in dozens of foundational Han-dynasty formulas (_Shanghan Lun_, _Jin Gui Yao Lue_); the principal *harmonizing* and *Spleen-Stomach-tonifying* food-drug of the Chinese tradition.#footnote[大棗 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (神農本草經), top grade; _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The harmonizing role is treated extensively in Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed., where 大棗 appears in the formula notes of dozens of classical decoctions.]

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Warm (溫); the *steam-processed black date* 黑棗 is treated as somewhat more warming than the standard red date.

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘 gān) — *intensely sweet*, in a register the classical tradition explicitly distinguishes from the lighter sweetness of jujube-related grain or root drugs.

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃) — primary; with secondary action on Heart (心), Lung (肺).

*Actions & indications:*
- *補中益氣 (bǔ zhōng yì qì)* — tonifies the middle, augments qi. The cardinal action: 大棗 is a Spleen-Stomach qi tonic, used widely for fatigue, poor appetite, weak digestion, and as a supportive food-medicine in convalescence.
- *養血安神 (yǎng xuè ān shén)* — nourishes blood and calms the spirit (mild). The classical _Jin Gui_ formula *甘麥大棗湯* (Gān Mài Dà Zǎo Tāng, "Licorice Wheat Jujube Decoction") — for _zàngzào_ (restless-organ disorder, female emotional lability) — is the canonical spirit-calming use; jujube here pairs with wheat (浮小麥) and honey-fried licorice.
- *調和諸藥 / 緩和藥性 (tiáo hé zhū yào / huǎn hé yào xìng)* — *harmonizes the actions of other herbs / moderates drug properties*. This is the *signature classical function* of 大棗 in formulas: it is added to dozens of Han-dynasty decoctions specifically to *protect the Spleen-Stomach from the harsh action of other ingredients* and to *harmonize* a formula's properties — used with strong-cathartic herbs (e.g. 十棗湯 Shí Zǎo Tāng, "Ten Jujube Decoction," with the harsh _kansui_/euphorbia diuretics), with strong-warming herbs (e.g. with ephedra in _Mahuang Tang_), and with virtually any formula that includes harsh ingredients. The harmonizing role is the principal reason 大棗 appears in formulas — and the role for which the herb is most distinctive across the materia medica.
- *Dietetic use:* daily inclusion of 大棗 in soups and congees as a Spleen-Stomach tonic and qi-blood support; the South Chinese 紅棗茶 (jujube tea, often with longan, goji, ginger) is a household tonic register.

*Cautions:* Damp-heat / phlegm-damp accumulation patterns; food stagnation; severe heat-pattern conditions (the warming-sweetening action is contraindicated); diabetes / blood-sugar concerns at heavy dietary doses (the high sugar content matters in the modern register); excessive use can be cloying and damp-generating.#footnote[Actions, cautions, and formula uses: 大棗 entries in _Bencao Gangmu_ and Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; harmonizing role is discussed extensively in Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, in the formula-note sections.]

*Note on 酸棗仁 (suānzǎorén, sour jujube seed) — a sibling drug:* the seed of the sour / thorny jujube _Z. jujuba_ var. _spinosa_ is a *distinct canonical drug*, neutral-sour-sweet (中性 / 酸 / 甘), entering the Heart (心), Liver (肝), Gallbladder (膽), and Spleen (脾) channels — *not* the Spleen-Stomach focus of 大棗 — and used principally for *insomnia and blood-vacuity restlessness* (the action of *養心安神*), in the foundational formula *酸棗仁湯* (_Suān Zǎo Rén Tāng_, "Sour Jujube Decoction" — _Jin Gui Yao Lue_). It is the principal Chinese herbal hypnotic, with documented modern phytopharmacology (jujuboside, betulinic acid). _Suānzǎorén is a separate drug from 大棗 and a candidate for its own future entry._

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Badara_ (बदर) is classical, treated by Charaka in the _phala-varga_ (fruits section) of Sūtrasthāna 27 and in Suśruta and the Bhāvaprakāśa. The classical Indian _badara_ refers principally to *_Ziziphus mauritiana_* (the Indian ber, tropical/subtropical South Asia), with *_Z. jujuba_* (the temperate jujube of the north Indian foothills and central Asian foodscape) overlapping in classical reception. The reading below applies to both species, with notes on the small differences where the classical commentators distinguish them.#footnote[Badara in Charaka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 (_phala-varga_); Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 46; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Āmrādi-phala-varga_; P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II, _badara_ entry. The Z. mauritiana / Z. jujuba overlap in classical commentary is recognized in modern dravyaguna literature.]

*Rasa (taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet) — predominant; *Amla* (अम्ल, sour) — present in unripe and partly-ripe fruit, fading on full ripening and drying. The ripe and dried fruit is read as more sweetly and less sourly than the green / underripe.

*Guṇa (qualities):* Guru (गुरु, heavy), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, unctuous) — when fresh and well-ripened. *Laghu* (लघु, light) and slightly *rūkṣa* (rough) when dried in some readings; the dried fruit becomes denser and more concentrated.

*Vīrya (potency):* Śīta (शीत, cooling) — when ripe and fresh; *Uṣṇa* (उष्ण, warming) when dried and concentrated, per some commentators (Bhāvaprakāśa). The fresh / dry distinction is real and is paralleled in the Chinese 紅棗 / 黑棗 register.

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of badara: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Āmrādi-phala-varga_; P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II. The fresh / dried distinction parallels the Chinese 大棗 / 黑棗 distinction — a real cross-system convergence on the same plant.]

*Prabhāva:* No distinct prabhāva is assigned in the core saṃhitās. The *harmonizing / agni-protective* action — strongly developed in the Chinese tradition — is not given a parallel prabhāva-level treatment in the Indian classical literature, where badara is treated more as a nutritive sweet fruit and less as a harmonizer-of-formulas. The classical Indian _badara_ reading is mostly _balya_ / _bṛṃhaṇa_ — strengthening, anabolic — and does not develop the Chinese formula-harmonizing role as a distinct prabhāva.

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies Vāta and Pitta (vāta-pitta-śāmaka) when ripe — sweet-cooling-snigdha is the classical anabolic Vāta-Pitta-pacifying register; increases Kapha at heavy dose (heavy-sweet-cooling) — the standard Kapha-aggravation pattern for sweet heavy fruits. Mildly increases Vāta in deeply unripe / sour state.

*Karma (actions):*
- _Balya_ — strengthening; the classical _balya phala_ register.
- _Bṛṃhaṇa_ — bulk / anabolic / mass-building.
- _Hṛdya_ — cardiotonic.
- _Tarpaṇa_ — satisfying / replenishing.
- _Śleṣmala_ — Kapha-generating (when in excess, the standard caveat).
- _Vṛṣya_ — mild aphrodisiac / reproductive-tissue support (in some readings).
- _Tṛṣṇāhara_ — relieves thirst (the cooling-sweet register).
- _Dāhahara_ — relieves burning sensation.
- _Used in:_ _kārśya_ (emaciation), _kṣaya_ (wasting), convalescence; _amlapitta_ (mild Pitta-pacifying); _atisāra_ (in dried form for sweet astringent diarrhea correction); _tṛṣṇā_ (thirst).

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(S) Conditional (doṣa / avasthā) viruddha:* heavy fresh badara in Kapha excess, _āma_, obesity (_sthaulya_), _prameha_ (diabetes-class disorders), low-_agni_ states — the standard Kapha-aggravation caution for heavy-sweet fruits.
- *(N) Combination cautions:* heavy badara preparations with milk and curd (sweet-snigdha + sweet-snigdha + sweet-snigdha-amla) in deep Kapha excess; the general fruit-with-milk caveat applies, though in the case of badara the classical sources are not uniform.
- *(F) Folk:* underripe badara is treated as digestively heavy and as a cause of _ādhmāna_ (gas) in folk practice — eat only well-ripened.
- *(M) Modern medical:*
  - *Diabetes / glycemic load:* dried jujubes / red dates are *very high in concentrated fructose-sucrose*, with a significant glycemic response in standard servings; the modern diabetic register is to limit heavy daily intake. (The classical Chinese 紅棗茶 practice should be modified for diabetic patients — small amounts only.)
  - *Anticoagulant interactions (theoretical):* in vitro evidence is limited; clinically modest concern.
  - *Allergy:* Rhamnaceae allergy is uncommon but documented.
  - *Pesticide residues* in commercial Chinese 大棗: the modern supply chain has occasional residue issues — flagged for sourcing, with organic / regulated sources preferable.#footnote[Conditional doṣa cautions: standard dravyaguna principle for guru-snigdha fruits per Bhāvaprakāśa. (M) glycemic load: standard nutrition data — dried jujubes have ~70–80% sugar by weight; relevant for diabetic management. The Rhamnaceae allergy register is small but documented in allergy literature.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_ʿUnnāb_ (عُنّاب) is *canonical* in Tibb — Avicenna treats it in _al-Qānūn_ Book II as a major drug, with extensive discussion of mizāj and therapeutic uses; Ibn al-Bayṭār treats it in _al-Jāmiʿ_; the herb is widely used in Unani _sharbat_, _maʿjūn_, and _ʿaraqiyāt_ preparations. The genus is also Qurʾānic — *_sidr_* (سدر, *_Ziziphus spina-christi_*, "Christ's thorn jujube") is the tree of the _Sidrat al-Muntahā_ (سدرة المنتهى, "the Lote-tree at the boundary [of paradise]") in Sūrat al-Najm 53:14–16 and Sūrat al-Wāqiʿah 56:28 — giving the genus a *Ṭibb al-Nabawī* register through the closely related species. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ` for the framework.

*Arabic:* عُنّاب (_ʿunnāb_) — the canonical Tibb name for _Z. jujuba_ and _Z. spina-christi_ in the medicinal register; سدر (_sidr_) — the sacred-Quranic name for _Z. spina-christi_ specifically, used for the leaves (washing the dead, ritual ablution use). The fruit of _sidr_ is نبق (_nabq_) in Arabian usage, a beloved Arabian desert fruit. Persian: عُنّاب (_ʿannāb_); Urdu: عناب (_unnāb_).

*Mizāj:* *Cold and Dry in the 1st degree* per Avicenna for _ʿunnāb_ (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II); some Persian and Maghrebi sources read it as *cold and moist in the 1st degree*, on the basis of the demulcent throat-and-chest use. The *cold* classification — like the Indian _śīta vīrya_ — converges across Tibb, Indian, and Chinese (where 大棗 is read as 溫 warm — a divergence to flag explicitly). The cross-system divergence on jujube warming/cooling is one of the cleaner classical disagreements: *Avicennan Tibb cold; Indian Ayurveda cooling-when-ripe; Chinese warming*. The reading-direction may track the freshness/dryness of the form most familiar to each tradition (Tibb texts often discuss the fresh fruit; Chinese pharmacy uses the dried form).

*Afʿāl (actions):*
- *Mulayyin* — mildly laxative.
- *Munaḍḍif li-l-ṣadr* (نظف الصدر) — *chest-cleansing* — the cardinal Tibb action; for cough, chest congestion, throat irritation, hoarseness.
- *Munaqqī al-dam* — blood-purifying. The canonical Tibb register treats _ʿunnāb_ as a blood-cleanser — used in compound preparations for skin disorders, blood-heat, and "thick blood" (high cholesterol / atheroma in modern Unani usage).
- *Mufattiḥ li-l-suddad* — mild deobstruent.
- *Musakkin* — sedative / soothing (mild).
- *Ghidhāʾ ḥasan* (غذاء حسن) — *good nutrition* / wholesome food; classical Tibb register for nutritive food-medicines.

*Istemālāt (indications):* cough; sore throat; hoarseness; chest tightness; blood-heat skin conditions; constipation (mild); convalescence; general nutritive use; sherbet base.

*Mawāneʿ / Iḥtirāzāt:* heavy cold-phlegm patterns; diabetes (high natural sugar); diarrhea (the moistening effect); excessive use in cold-stomach patterns.

*Muṣliḥ:* honey; ginger; cinnamon — to balance the cold quality where the patient's mizāj calls for warming.

*Badal:* fig (_tīn_); raisin (_zabīb_); _sebestens_ (_sapistān_, _Cordia myxa_) — the latter being a closely-paralleled chest-cleansing fruit in the Tibb register.

*Classical Tibb formulas:*
- *Sharbat al-ʿUnnāb* (شربت العناب) — the canonical jujube sherbet, a syrup of jujube extract with sugar / honey, taken as a chest-cleansing and blood-purifying preparation; one of the most familiar of the Tibb _sharbat_ class. Standard Unani / Hakim's pharmacy preparation.
- *Joshānda* (Persian / Unani decoctions for cough and chest disorders) — _ʿunnāb_ is a standard ingredient, paired with _sapistān_ (_Cordia myxa_), _khatmī_ (marshmallow), _banafsha_ (violet), _maghz-i bādām_ (almond paste) — the classical seven-flower / seven-fruit chest-cleansing compound.
- *Maʿjūn-i Mufarriḥ* and similar — _ʿunnāb_ appears as a supporting nutritive in mood-elevating electuary preparations.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* *The genus is Qurʾānic.* _Z. spina-christi_ is the *sidr* tree of *Sūrat al-Najm 53:14–16* — "_ʿinda Sidrat al-Muntahā / ʿindahā Jannat al-Maʾwā_" ("at the Lote-tree of the boundary, by which is the Garden of Refuge") — describing the Prophet's Night Journey ascent. Sūrat al-Wāqiʿah 56:28 references "_sidrin makhḍūd_" ("thornless _sidr_") in the description of the people of the right. *Sidr leaves* are mentioned in canonical hadith as used for washing the dead (multiple narrations, esp. on the washing of the Prophet's daughter Zaynab — Bukhārī, Muslim) and in some traditions for ruqyah (Qurʾānic protective recitation with water). The *fruit (nabq)* is an attested food of the Companions in Madinan agricultural reports. _ʿUnnāb_ specifically is referenced in the Prophetic-medicine compendia (Ibn Qayyim al-Jawziyya, _al-Ṭibb al-Nabawī_) as a chest-cleansing food-medicine, drawing on the broader Tibb register.#footnote[Tibb _ʿunnāb_: Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn fī al-Ṭibb_, Book II (s.v. _ʿunnāb_); Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_ (s.v. _ʿunnāb_, _sidr_); _Khazāyin al-Adwiya_, vol. III, _ʿunnāb_ entry. Quranic _sidr_: Sūrat al-Najm 53:14–16; Sūrat al-Wāqiʿah 56:28. Sidr-leaf hadiths on washing the dead: Bukhārī (_Kitāb al-Janāʾiz_), Muslim. Ibn Qayyim, _al-Ṭibb al-Nabawī_ — _ʿunnāb_ entry. The cross-system mizāj divergence (Tibb cold, Chinese warm, Ayurvedic cooling-when-ripe) is real and is one of the cleaner classical disagreements.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *甘麥大棗湯 (Gān Mài Dà Zǎo Tāng, "Licorice Wheat Jujube Decoction")* — _Jin Gui Yao Lue_; for _zàngzào_ (restless-organ disorder); 甘草 + 浮小麥 + 大棗.
- *十棗湯 (Shí Zǎo Tāng, "Ten Jujube Decoction")* — _Shanghan Lun_ / _Jin Gui_; for pleural / peritoneal water-collection; the harsh herbs (甘遂 _kansui_, 大戟 _euphorbia_, 芫花 _genkwa_) are *taken with a jujube decoction* to protect the Spleen-Stomach — a paradigmatic case of jujube's harmonizing-protective role.
- *小建中湯 (Xiǎo Jiàn Zhōng Tāng, "Minor Construct-the-Middle Decoction")* — _Shanghan Lun_; for Spleen-Stomach vacuity cold abdominal pain; 桂枝 + 芍藥 + 甘草 + 生薑 + 大棗 + 飴糖 (malt sugar).
- *桂枝湯 (Guì Zhī Tāng, "Cinnamon Twig Decoction")* — _Shanghan Lun_; the canonical wind-strike / surface-harmonizing formula; jujube as Spleen-Stomach support.
- *大棗 in dozens of other formulas* — wherever harsh, cathartic, strongly-cold, or strongly-warming herbs need Spleen-Stomach protection.
- *Dietetic preparations:* 紅棗粥 (jujube congee), 紅棗茶 (jujube tea — often with longan, goji, ginger), 紅棗湯 (jujube soup), 阿膠紅棗膏 (donkey-hide gelatin + jujube paste, the canonical female blood-tonic).#footnote[Formula sources: Zhang Zhongjing, _Shanghan Lun_ and _Jin Gui Yao Lue_; Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. The harmonizing role across formulas is the clinical center of gravity for 大棗.]

=== Ayurveda

- *Badara as fresh fruit / dietetic use* — the classical Indian register is mostly dietetic; _badara_ is a sweet nourishing fruit eaten in season for _balya / bṛṃhaṇa_.
- *Badara cūrṇa, badara rasāyana* — dried-jujube preparations in the rasāyana register, particularly in north Indian and Himalayan traditions.
- *Badara-pradhāna kṣīrapāka* — milk decoctions with jujube as a nutritive tonic.
- *Karkandhu cūrṇa* — preparations with the smaller-fruited variety, used dietetically as a digestive sweet.
- *Mukhavāsa register:* dried jujube is a traditional post-meal sweet in some north Indian regional cuisines.#footnote[Badara preparations: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu and the standard Ayurvedic dietary literature. The Indian classical use is more dietetic than formulary, in contrast to the Chinese pharmacy-central role.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

- *Greco-Roman:* Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ I.118 (_zízyphon_) treats the jujube as a chest-cleansing, demulcent, throat-soothing fruit — *the same chest-cleansing reading as the Tibb _munaḍḍif li-l-ṣadr_*, reflecting the shared Hellenistic-Galenic-Arabic synthesis. Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XV; Galen on jujube as chest-clearing.
- *Medieval European:* jujube paste (Ital. _giuggiola_, the lozenge-and-syrup register) becomes the canonical European cough lozenge — the term *jujube* in English-language pharmacy comes to mean *medicated cough lozenge* in the 18th–19th c., regardless of whether actual jujube fruit is present (the word survives as a candy-name into the 20th c. as the "Jujube" pastille).
- *Mediterranean cultivation and culinary use:* extensive — Sicily, Greece, southern Italy, southern Spain, the Levant. *Bagolaro* and *giuggiole* tradition in Italy; *Bevanda dei sette frutti* (the seven-fruit chest-clearing decoction) of Mediterranean folk pharmacy parallels the Unani _joshānda_.
- *Modern Western herbal:* jujube tea and jujube extract preparations for cough and chest congestion persist in modern phytotherapy; ESCOP does not have a dedicated _Ziziphi fructus_ monograph, but the herb is in the European herbal tradition.#footnote[Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ I.118 — Beck translation; Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XV (Loeb / Rackham); Galen on jujube. Medieval European jujube-lozenge tradition: _Oxford English Dictionary_ on "jujube" as confectionery / lozenge. Italian and Mediterranean culinary use: standard regional gastronomy.]

=== Ṭibb (Arabic-Islamic medicine)

- *Sharbat al-ʿUnnāb* — canonical jujube sherbet (covered above).
- *Joshānda-yi mufradāt* (seven-fruit chest decoction) — _ʿunnāb_ + _sapistān_ (Cordia myxa) + _khatmī_ (marshmallow) + _banafsha_ (violet) + _bādām_ (almond) + _injīr_ (fig) + _maghz-i tukhm-i kharbūza_ (melon-seed) — the canonical Unani chest-cleansing decoction; one of the great Tibb compound formulations.
- *Itrīfal* preparations of various types — _ʿunnāb_ as a supporting ingredient in some compound mukhavāsa-class electuaries.
- *Maʿjūn-i Mufarriḥ* — mood-elevating electuary register; _ʿunnāb_ as nutritive.
- *Sidr leaf preparations* — in traditional Arabian and South Asian practice, _sidr_ leaves are used for washing hair (a beloved Arabian tradition, paralleled with henna), and traditionally for ritual washing of the deceased per the hadith narrations; in folk medicine, sidr leaves are used externally for skin complaints and as a wound-wash.#footnote[Tibb formulas: standard Unani formulary; Hakim Najmul Ghani Khan, _Khazāyin al-Adwiya_, vol. III; AYUSH _National Formulary of Unani Medicine_; CCRUM publications. The seven-fruit Joshānda is one of the most-recognized Unani chest formulations.]

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

_Ziziphus jujuba_ is native to northern China and Central Asia — a *cold-tolerant warm-temperate continental* tree, with adaptation to harsh continental winters (-30 °C in deep dormancy) and hot dry summers; it is also cultivated extensively across the Mediterranean basin, the Middle East, and the Himalayan foothills. The closely related _Z. spina-christi_ is a hot-arid desert / thorn-savannah species (Arabian peninsula, Saharan fringe), and _Z. mauritiana_ a hot tropical / sub-tropical species (South Asia, sub-Saharan Africa). The dietetic role of jujube tracks each climate distinctly, with the medicinal-pharmacy register most strongly elaborated in the *continental Chinese* and *Middle Eastern Tibb* traditions. See `climates.typ` for the foundational framework.

=== Continental seasonal-extreme (northern China, Korea, Central Asia) — the canonical Chinese climate

Hot-humid summer, cold-dry winter; constitutional swing across the year is wide; Spleen-yang chronically loaded in winter, Pitta-Kapha pressure in summer. _Ziziphus jujuba_'s harvest in the dry autumn fits the late-_xià_ / early-_qiū_ climatic moment of Spleen-Earth, and the dried fruit is a *winter nutritive staple* in the canonical north Chinese register.

*Traditional preparation.* 紅棗 / 大棗 as the year-round Spleen-Stomach tonic — added to soups (老火湯 _lǎo huǒ tāng_, slow-cooked Cantonese soups; northern Chinese winter stews), congees (紅棗粥), warming teas (with longan, goji, ginger), and as a winter snack. The *steam-processing* into 黑棗 produces the warming-concentrated form for deep winter and for Spleen-yang-vacuity patients.

*Cares.* The jujube's warming-sweet-tonifying profile is climatically *correct* for the dry-cold winter of the continental zone and as a *between-season harmonizing food* for the seasonal transitions. Heavy daily intake in *hot-humid summer* would compound damp-heat — the classical dietetic register accordingly emphasizes winter / cool-season heavy use and moderation in summer.

=== Warm-dry Mediterranean / Middle Eastern — the canonical Tibb climate

Hot dry summer, cool wet winter; Pitta-Vāta pressure in summer, Kapha-Vāta in winter; external 燥 + 熱 (dryness + heat) in summer.

*Traditional preparation.* The Mediterranean and Levantine register treats jujube as a *summer cooling-demulcent fruit* — the Tibb _ʿunnāb_ reading as *cold-and-dry / cold-and-moist* aligns precisely with this climatic use. *Sharbat al-ʿUnnāb* — the jujube sherbet — is a canonical Levantine and Persian *summer* drink, served chilled-or-room-temperature, as a chest-cleansing, blood-cooling refreshment. *Joshānda-yi mufradāt* (the seven-fruit chest decoction) is a *winter cough-and-cold remedy*. The seasonal split — summer sherbet vs. winter decoction — is the classical _kāla_-pattern preserved through the modern Tibb tradition.

*Cares.* In hot-dry climate, the *fresh-fruit and sherbet* register is climatically apt; the dried-and-warming preparations should be reserved for cool months or used in compound preparations balanced with cooling agents (rose-water, sandalwood, lemon).

=== Hot-arid Arabian (Z. spina-christi, sidr) — Quranic-sacred climate

Hot, dry, sparse-rain desert and thorn-savannah; Pitta-Vāta with strong _rasakṣaya_ (fluid depletion); external 燥 (dryness).

*Traditional preparation.* The _sidr_ tree is a *survival tree* of the Arabian desert — its fruit (_nabq_) is a beloved seasonal food, its leaves used for hair-washing (a longstanding Arabian and Yemeni tradition), and the wood used for cooking and incense in some regional uses. The *sidr honey* (_ʿasal al-sidr_) — produced by bees foraging on sidr nectar in Yemen, Hadramawt, and parts of Oman and Saudi Arabia — is one of the world's most prized and expensive honeys, with a distinct flavor and dense-amber color. The Quranic-sacred status of the sidr tree intersects with this everyday desert food role in a manner unusual in the materia medica.

*Cares.* Sidr leaves and fruit are climatically apt for the desert pattern — cooling, demulcent, blood-cleansing. The hot-dry-climate dietetic register favors them; the Quranic-sacred status carries through into Muslim dietary practice across climates as a desirable food (the *sidr honey* trade is global precisely on this strength).

=== Hot tropical (Z. mauritiana, badara / ber) — South Asian classical climate

Hot-humid monsoonal; Pitta-Kapha pressure; external 濕熱 (damp-heat).

*Traditional preparation.* The Indian _ber_ is a household tropical fruit — eaten fresh in season (early summer in north India), dried and powdered (_bori_ in some regional usages), made into chutney, sun-dried into preserved snack-food; the classical Ayurvedic register treats it as a sweet-cooling fruit good for thirst and convalescence.

*Cares.* In hot-humid climate, the fresh-ripe register is climatically apt; the unripe / sour register is folk-cautioned as digestively heavy.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_)

The modern globalized health-food register has moved 大棗 / red dates into smoothies, cold extracts, and chilled tea concentrates across all climates — the warm-and-tonifying Chinese register transposed to climates and seasons where its constitutional logic is reversed. Cold jujube extract on ice in hot-humid Mumbai or São Paulo is *non-corrective* in the classical reading — not toxic, but no longer doing the climatic-constitutional work of the original Chinese dietetic register. The traditional register is *warm decoction or congee in cool-season continental climate*. The Tibb summer-sherbet register is climatically apt for warm-dry Mediterranean. *Pairing register to climate is the recovery move*; the _deśa-viruddha_ category from Caraka Sū. 26 names the mismatch.#footnote[Climate framework: see `climates.typ`. Northern Chinese dietetic patterns: Kastner, _Chinese Nutrition Therapy_; Anderson, _The Food of China_. Tibb _ʿunnāb_ sherbet register: standard Unani dietetic literature. Sidr honey: regional specialty literature.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Approximate composition (dried red date / Chinese jujube, per 100 g):*
- Energy ~290 kcal; Water ~15 g
- Carbohydrate ~73 g (~65 g sugar — predominantly fructose and glucose, with sucrose); fiber ~6 g
- Protein ~3 g; Fat ~1 g
- Minerals: potassium very high (~530 mg), magnesium ~37 mg, calcium ~30 mg, iron ~1.5 mg, zinc, manganese
- Vitamin C: *very high in fresh fruit* (~70–80 mg per 100 g fresh; declines substantially on drying)
- Polysaccharides — a major bioactive constituent of jujube fruit; cluster of jujube polysaccharides (ZJP) with documented immunomodulatory and antioxidant activity
- Triterpene saponins — jujubosides, jujuboside A and B (highest concentration in the seed of _Z. spinosa_, lower in the fruit of _Z. jujuba_)
- Flavonoids: spinosin, vicenin, swertish
- Phenolic acids: chlorogenic acid, caffeic acid, ferulic acid
- Cyclic AMP and cyclic GMP — present in jujube fruit at relatively high levels (a notable phytochemical feature; relevance to the calming and Spleen-tonifying actions is hypothesized)#footnote[Phytochemistry of _Z. jujuba_ fruit: standard pharmacognosy; recent reviews in _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_, _Food Chemistry_, _Phytochemistry Reviews_. The cAMP/cGMP content of jujube is one of the more distinctive phytochemical features and is sometimes cited in the modern Chinese pharmacology literature on the calming-tonifying actions.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Immunomodulation* — jujube polysaccharides show immunomodulatory activity preclinically (macrophage activation, NK-cell support); supports the classical _bǔ qì_ / qi-tonifying register.
- *Hepatoprotective* — preclinical hepatoprotective activity in chemical injury models; modest clinical evidence.
- *Antioxidant* — high vitamin C, polyphenols, flavonoids; the modern "antioxidant superfood" reputation is supported phytochemically but should not be overstated clinically.
- *Sedative / anxiolytic* — modest activity from the saponin fraction; the seed of sour jujube (酸棗仁) is the principal sedative, with much higher saponin content; jujube fruit (大棗) is a milder cousin of this register.
- *Anti-anaemic (modest)* — the iron content is modest, not high; the classical "blood-tonifying" reading is *moderate* in modern terms — the herb is more a qi-tonic than a true blood-tonic in modern hematological terms.
- *Glycemic load* — *significant*: dried jujubes are ~65% sugar by weight; the glycemic index of dried jujube is moderate-high; for diabetic patients, this is the principal modern caveat against heavy use.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Dietetic / culinary register:* 3–10 dried jujubes per day (~10–30 g) is the standard Chinese dietetic intake; no toxicological concern at this level in non-diabetic populations.
- *Pharmacologic decoction:* 3–12 g per day in compound formulas (TCM standard range); occasionally higher in specific formulas (十棗湯 uses very large amounts).
- *Diabetic / pre-diabetic:* significant reduction — the high sugar content is the limiting factor; 1–2 dates per day or less, with monitoring.
- *Pregnancy:* no specific contraindication; culinary use is safe.
- *Infants and children:* whole jujubes are a choking hazard for young children; jujube tea is generally safe.
- *Sourcing:* organic / certified jujubes are preferable for chronic use, given documented pesticide residue issues in some commercial Chinese supply chains.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Dose ranges: Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; standard TCM pharmacology references; glycemic data from standard nutrition databases. Diabetic considerations are modern medical, not classical — verify against current diabetology guidelines.]

